 you  take a bunch of two clicks like we have  here at the top and connect them in this  way and you have  arrows going forward from one to click  to the next  it turns out that all of these two  clicks will have surviving uh stable six  points and so this  tlm network has um you know six stable  fixed points one per two click  however if you give a pulse to the so if  you you know initi depending on initial  conditions you'll go to one fixed point  or another but if you start for instance  at the blue the light blue dark blue  fixed point and you give a pulse to the  whole network  so you can see um down below we have  these this pulse train  uh this global pulse to the whole  network will actually  kick the activity over into the next  stable fixed point so go from  say blue to green  and then from green to  pink and red and so on  and so this uh  based on the on the theoretical results  we're able to define to design an  extremely simple  counter network that will keep track of  how many pulses have come in based on  the the attractor that the network is in  where every pulse bumps you over  okay and keep in mind these are global  pulses they are not targeting specific  fixed points it's just like it's moving  it over every time  and we can have similarly a signed  counter where  you can have left and right pulses the  left pulses would stimulate the bottom  row of neurons the right pulses would  stimulate the top row of neurons and  similarly you can have then the activity  moving  left or right in this network  depending on pulses coming from the left  or the right and so for instance when  you have these experiments where an  animal is  running down a corridor and  counting cues on the left versus on the  right right this is a simple network  that would keep track of  um the net sign  that the the net number of you know left  minus right cues  okay and then we have another this is  kind of a fun tln roulette which is  if you get rid of some of the edges you  can get the activity to spin around and  kind of stop on a random  two click  okay so let me give you another example  if you think about place cell coding in  the hippocampus uh one thing that's very  interesting is when you zoom in and look  within  a cycle of the of the theta oscillation  you actually see neurons firing in  sequence not at stable fixed points so  this again suggests that maybe  periodic or dynamic attractors can be a  good model for position in this context  and so  again using that same theorem we talked  about before but now applying it to this  more complicated graph we have a nice  rule graphically knowing when the fixed  point will survive and then predicting  when the attractor will survive  and so we can come up with um  what i call a continuous in quotes  attractor network because it's not  really a continuum of attractors but it  is um a sort of a discrete family of  many attractors that are all of the same  type so there's this modularity to the  to the structure of the network  and what you see with the network like  this is from the mathematical theory we  can predict that we will have uh limit  cycles for each of these  pentagonal  sub networks  and stimulation can can move you from  from one to another  and moreover you can have a situation  where a global stimulation uh where  you're hitting sort of all of these red  nodes  you know one per attractor so the the  stimulus doesn't you know know where the  position is doesn't know where you are  it's just hitting the whole network um  at once and it's always the same  so these identical theta pulses that are  hitting you know this distributed set of  red nodes will again like in the counter  case move you from one attractor to the  next this is another mechanism by which  you can have  position tracking  um in a in a sort of more complex  network where internally the attractors  are not fixed points this time that they  are  sequential right so they are dynamic  attractors and the neurons are firing in  sequence  and you can even add some noise  to  the matrix and you can see that the  noise  can cause some failures of transitions  but overall it's quite robust and so  again you have kind of these global  theta pulses  that can move you  from one attractor to the next even  though the external post knows nothing  about the current  position of the of the network activity  within the family of attractors  okay and uh  finally maybe i'll just take a few more  minutes to talk about  one more theorem  which is about cyclic unions so it turns  out  there are many theorems that we have  where we can predict  the fixed points of a network that is  generated by gluing together  smaller networks okay and so in this  case the structure is a cyclic union so  you have smaller networks taiwan tau two  etc i'm not showing the connections  within those but then they're glued  together in the cyclic fashion having  all the edges going forward from one  component to the next but none of the  back edges  and in this setup we have this nice  theorem  that tells us that the fixed points of  the larger network  are basically unions of fixed points of  fixed point supports of the component  networks so in other words if you know  the fixed points for the individual  components you can work out the fixed  points for the whole network in a very  simple way  and  this is an example of actually doing  that so  it's a little complicated slide but  this is really showing that from working  out the fixed points of these individual  small networks and the blobs  we can figure out all the fixed points  for the entire network in two examples  okay so i'll end with an application of  this and  and that is to central pattern generator  circuits and in this case uh my student  juliana worked out a central pattern  generator circuit for quadruped gates  so these are the familiar you know like  for a horse walk trot you know gallop  bound pace um i'm not sure the horse  does pronk but these are all familiar  quadruped gates  so these are locomotive patterns of  motion  and  uh  what we  found or what leanna found was that she  could genera she could create using  cyclic unions  networks that that um  generated activity for each of these  gates so here's a network that does  bound so the the neurons representing  the  um  the legs of the horse or or the animal  are  two three one two three and four so left  front right front left back and right  back  um those are the four  the four legs for the quadruped motion  and um this is a cyclic union  architecture which is  and using this she was able to create  the bound gates  um  using uh  another cyclic union that still involved  those same four nodes but some other  different auxiliary nodes she was able  to  create pace  and so you can see down at the bottom  right the pattern of activity of when  those legs hit the ground right and so  you can see um  these different patterns they actually  if you if you trace back  to what the different gates are they  match up with what you would expect for  these gates so this is one for trot  uh this is one for walk  okay and and finally for prank and so  the point was that she was able to find  to design using cyclic unions individual  networks that individually created all  of these gates but of course there are  these same four neurons involved one two  three and four for the different legs  okay so the question then was can we put  these together in a single network where  all five of these gates would coexist  okay and be accessible via different  initial conditions without having to  change any other parameters  and uh what juliana found was that yes  you could actually do this  so she was able to sort of really just  glue together take sort of the union of  all the edges from each of these  different  networks that she constructed for the  gates and  it's a simple you know this is not  fine-tuned at all these are standard  choices of epsilon and delta  these are the two parameters for making  the w matrix from the graph if you  remember there is a recipe for going  from the graph to the cellular matrix  and down here at the bottom left is the  adjacency matrix for kind of this giant  graph but it's really  if you think about it a very simple  graph that's just obtained by gluing  together  the the graphs for the individual gates  and so it has 24 neurons  and you know here's the matrix for it  and remarkably  this graph with all of these individual  limits  all of these individual cyclic unions  glued together  actually produces all five of the gates  okay and it's not hard to find initial  conditions for each gate because you  really just need to stimulate one of the  neurons  that is sort of unique to that gate so  there are overlapping neurons for the  five gates but then there are neurons  that only appear in one gate so if you  stimulate one of those neurons  you actually end up  in  in the appropriate gate and just want to  emphasize one more time the only  difference here is initial conditions  so in fact this means that you could  transition from one gate to another by  kicking the network  which effectively  you know resets the initial condition to  a different part of the state space so  here is an example  where i started in the bound gate so  there's some pattern of activity  of the of the feet hitting the ground  the legs hitting the ground for the  bound gate and then a little kick so  this is one neuron neuron 15  which is one of the unique neurons to  pace got stimulated here and that kicked  the activity from bound into pace you  can see there's a different pattern of  activity  in the in the pink rectangle here this  is the focus on the four neurons that  are actually determining the gate the  four neurons for the legs  and then another stimulation neuron 19  which is unique to walk kicks you into  the walk gate  and then neuron 23 kicks you into prank  neuron 17 kicks you into trot and so  here we see that not only do these gates  coexist in the same network but it's  very easy to transition between them  just by stimulating single neurons that  that will activate the different gates  and so this gives you a very clean  mechanism by which a central pattern  generator circuit a single central  pattern generator circuit can can encode  multiple gates and this is a  we're very excited to see this because  um  in the  in the literature  on these  types of models usually  uh one has to change parameters to go  from one gate to another  and so this having the attractors truly  coexist without any parameter changes  you know exact same connectivity matrix  as we think new is a new um discovery  that this can be done  and then we've added also noise to the w  matrix to see that there's some  robustness here um in the architecture  one percent noise and the w matrix  didn't um alter the dynamics at all  five percent noise and we did see a  transition failure  from  between some of the gates  in this last this last transition  so it does break down at some point  and i think i want to end there um so  i'll just  show my collaborators again and  thank you very much for attending my  virtual talk
 

